Make an unfilled submodule loud, before the -enough family starts leaving

Adopts the petition `extract-the-enough-family.md` and does the half that has to
come **first**. No code moves in this PR. What changes is that this repository can
survive having a submodule, which today it cannot.

## Why this is the first PR and not the second

A folder and a submodule are byte-identical to an importer — `../git-enough/read.mjs`
resolves the same either way — which is what makes the whole extraction affordable.
It is also why the failure mode is so quiet: **nothing changes until a mount is
empty, and then everything changes at once, with no error message.**

Two defaults here compose into exactly that:

1. **`scripts/test.mjs` skipped a directory it could not find.**

       try { entries = readdirSync(join(root, d)); } catch { continue; }

   Right for an optional folder, wrong for a submodule. An unfilled `jekyll-enough`
   made four suites vanish and the run still printed `N/N passed`. A green check for
   a build that tested less than it did yesterday is the one failure nobody
   investigates.

2. **Neither workflow fetched submodules.** `actions/checkout@v4` does not unless
   asked. Compose that with #1 and you get a build that silently stops testing.

And a third that is worse than either, because it is user-visible:

3. **`pages-cloudflare.yml` runs `pages deploy .`** — it uploads the working tree.
   An unfilled mount publishes an *empty directory* into the live site. `viewer/`
   imports `git-enough/read.mjs` and `sw.js` caches it, so the browser 404s on a
   deploy that reported success.

So: the runner now **fails** on a missing listed directory and names the fix, and
both workflows hydrate. None of this is speculative — it is what would have broken,
found by doing the extraction and looking back.

## What else is in here

`docs/origin.md` gains **The family gets addresses**, which is what the petition
asked to land: the boundary table and the order.

The finding that most changes what "extract git-enough" means is that the folder is
**at least four things wearing one name** — git transport, a workflow runner
(`docs/actions-enough.md` already calls it `actions-enough`), a scheduler that is
already `cron-enough` in everything but name, and shims for `node:*` that should be
called **`node-enough`, not `shell-enough`**: there is no `ls`, no `grep`, no
pipeline in there, and in this family the lowercase word has to read as the thing it
replaces. One-repo-per-folder would ship a `git-enough` containing a scheduler.

Two things in it need you rather than me:

- **The bottle cluster is bigger than the petition thought.** *Leave them behind*
  over *invert the dependency* — `bottle.mjs` and `seize.mjs` are this repository's
  bottle and attestation concerns using git plumbing, not the reverse, and
  `composer/` already holds `bottle-attest`, `bottle-embed` and `bottle-uri`. But it
  is not two files: `bottle-boot.mjs` imports `serveOnHello` from `bottle.mjs` and
  **`bottle-boot.mjs` is served** — `probe-test/glove.ui.test.mjs` boots a page
  against `/git-enough/bottle-boot.mjs`. Moving only `bottle.mjs` relocates the
  outward import instead of removing it. The clean cut takes `bottle.mjs`,
  `bottle-boot.mjs`, `bottle-inception.mjs`, `bottle.html` and `seize.mjs`, which
  **changes a served URL path.** Named here, not done.
- **`yaml-enough` is a merge, not an extraction.** `jekyll-enough/yaml.mjs` (172
  lines) and `advocate.anecdote.channel/bin/yaml-enough.mjs` (144) are different code
  for the same job and the second already owns the name.

Also recorded because a first pass misses it: **the tests reach further than the
code.** `probe-ops.mjs` imports nothing outward and would extract without comment,
while `probe-ops.test.mjs` imports `composer/probe-line.mjs` and would stop running
the moment the folder moved. `composer/bottle-uri.mjs` appears in the test layer and
nowhere in the module graph. Survey both layers or ship a repository whose suite is
decoration.

## Verified

`node scripts/test.mjs` → **117/117 suites passed** on this branch.

The new failure path was tested by actually hiding the directory:

    $ mv jekyll-enough .je-hidden && node scripts/test.mjs
    test: 1 listed directory is missing: jekyll-enough
          If it is a submodule, it is not hydrated:  git submodule update --init --recursive
          In CI, that is `submodules: recursive` on actions/checkout.
          If it was renamed or removed, update the list in this file.
    exit=1

The workflow changes are not exercised until a run happens; they are two `with:`
blocks and the reasoning is in a comment beside each.

## Blocked, and it needs your hands

**`jekyll-enough` is already extracted** — 13 commits, authors intact, the 4 original
suites passing standalone plus a new `dependencies.test.mjs` that makes the family's
promise a test (a production module may import a sibling and nothing else; today the
four import zero `node:` builtins between them). It also carries `docs/mounting.md`,
the four silent-failure places written up from what would have broken here.

It is parked at **`~/Project/FCCN-ANTIBODY/jekyll-enough`** with its remote set and
nothing pushed, because **creating the GitHub repository was denied by this session's
permission classifier.** Create `FCCN-ANTIBODY/jekyll-enough` (public, no license —
matching `anecdote.channel` and `bottles.anecdote.channel`) or allow me to, and the
swap here is two commands: `git rm -r jekyll-enough` and `git submodule add`. The
hardening in this PR is what makes that swap safe, and it is worth landing either way.

## Not in scope

`civic-node` does **not** need a library engine for any of this — I checked, since it
came up. Its only references to the family are prose in `AGENTS.md` and
`OPEN-QUESTIONS.md`; no code consumes it and no workflow builds with it. The library
layer can name these and say where to get them, but it cannot put bytes in a build —
that is the consumer's job, which is the whole point of `docs/mounting.md`. A library
on `civic-node` is a real want and a separate one.
